00:33:13,640 --> 00:33:19,160
 specialises with the aging because the brain and the brain chemistry is so much different

449
00:33:19,160 --> 00:33:22,760
 when we're talking about a seventy or an eighty year old than we are when we're talking about

450
00:33:22,760 --> 00:33:23,680
 a forty year old.

451
00:33:27,320 --> 00:33:33,880
 Alzheimer's is a disease of dementia.

452
00:33:33,880 --> 00:33:36,320
 Now we have many diseases of dementia.

453
00:33:36,320 --> 00:33:41,600
 The reason that we bring Alzheimer's up front is because it seems to be one in which we

454
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:44,520
 have a lot more discussion about.

455
00:33:44,520 --> 00:33:49,800
 Now we do know that Alzheimer's can have two types early onset Alzheimer's which is a

456
00:33:49,800 --> 00:33:55,840
 genetic one that we can pretty much diagnose early on and then we have sort of the more

457
00:33:55,840 --> 00:34:00,360
 normal Alzheimer's or I want to say which comes on a little later in life.

458
00:34:00,360 --> 00:34:04,000
 Unfortunately right now we don't really have a great way to test whether or not you actually

459
00:34:04,000 --> 00:34:05,480
 have Alzheimer's.

460
00:34:05,480 --> 00:34:10,640
 And the other interesting thing is that when it comes to risk factors, there are some genes

461
00:34:10,640 --> 00:34:15,640
 that apparently hurt us and some genes that apparently help us.

462
00:34:15,640 --> 00:34:18,440
 So that's not so good either.

463
00:34:18,440 --> 00:34:24,640
 But the genes that we do know that tend to increase Alzheimer's are ones that we can

464
00:34:24,640 --> 00:34:25,760
 test for.

465
00:34:25,760 --> 00:34:31,280
 But remember we also tend to have genes that might reduce our risk factors so genes could

466
00:34:31,280 --> 00:34:35,160
 help or even hurt us.

467
00:34:35,160 --> 00:34:40,440
 So how do we know that somebody actually has Alzheimer's or only going to know after an

468
00:34:40,440 --> 00:34:42,360
 autopsy?

469
00:34:42,360 --> 00:34:48,640
 Unfortunately before that we don't really know if it's truly Alzheimer's or if it is

470
00:34:48,640 --> 00:34:53,440
 another type of dementia disease because there's more than one type of dementia disease that

471
00:34:53,440 --> 00:34:58,180
 might shrink this gray matter that we talk about so much.

472
00:34:58,180 --> 00:35:00,080
 So it is kind of unfortunate.

473
00:35:00,080 --> 00:35:04,640
 Now what we do most of the time these days is that we give it a try basically we look

474
00:35:04,640 --> 00:35:07,520
 at some neurological testing.

475
00:35:07,520 --> 00:35:08,720
 We do some medical testing.

476
00:35:08,720 --> 00:35:09,720
 We do some scans.

477
00:35:09,720 --> 00:35:12,600
 We look for behavior symptoms and then we give them medication.

478
00:35:12,600 --> 00:35:16,960
 If it seems to be working, we're going to assume it's Alzheimer's and we're going to

479
00:35:16,960 --> 00:35:20,200
 go forward from there.

480
00:35:20,200 --> 00:35:27,800
 Especially with dementia diseases the variety of them.

481
00:35:27,800 --> 00:35:31,600
 If the medication works for Alzheimer's it could also work for two or three other types

482
00:35:31,600 --> 00:35:33,480
 of dementia diseases.

483
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:42,480
 It is getting better at making those diagnoses as we begin to get better imaging.

484
00:35:42,480 --> 00:35:48,240
 The question though does come down to just how much do we really want to get into this

485
00:35:48,240 --> 00:35:50,700
 as far as the diagnosis is concerned.

486
00:35:50,700 --> 00:35:55,320
 What they're really looking at more is trying to find factors that can help stop Alzheimer's

487
00:35:55,320 --> 00:35:57,160
 or slow it down.

488
00:35:57,160 --> 00:36:02,020
 If we can slow down the Alzheimer's we may not be able to stop it because the brain is

489
00:36:02,020 --> 00:36:11,520
 going to age but slowing it down might allow someone to have a more successful senior life.

